From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 10:32:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] interconnect: apple: Add a PMP provider for fabric and DRAM
 requests

On t600x the PMP sets the fabric and DRAM controller (DCS) performance
states, from its own monitors and from the agents' DVFS requests in its
dashboard. Add an interconnect provider on top of the report driver's
DVFS request interface, so that drivers ask for memory bandwidth in the
usual way and the provider turns that into requests:

- one node per agent with a known slot (the three CPU clusters and the
  first video encoder on t6000/t6001), each linked to a DRAM node;
- an agent's aggregated bandwidth, the larger of average and peak,
  picks a step from a per-SoC table: up to 64 GB/s asks for fabric
  level 1, up to 128 GB/s for level 2, more for fabric level 3 and DCS
  level 3; no bandwidth withdraws the request;
- a request tag can add a minimum SoC level (the encoder's clock
  follows it).

The steps were measured on an M1 Max with CPU STREAM from one cluster's
slot: fabric level 1, 2 and 3+DCS 3 give about 128, 146 and 160 GB/s
(10 threads), against 55 GB/s with no request and 110 GB/s with the PMP
not running.

The report driver registers the provider as its child when the SoC's
slots are known. The provider is built in and cannot be unbound, since
consumers hold paths into it. t6002 is not described yet (two dies'
worth of clusters and agents) and gets no provider.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
